{{es-pr|+<audio:LL-Q1321 (spa)-AdrianAbdulBaha-marihuana.wav<a:Colombia>>}}

===Etymology 1===
{{etydate|1894}} of uncertain origin. Alteration by influence of the proper name {{m|es|María Juana||Mary Jane}} is believed to be {{glossary|folk etymology}}. According to the ''[[w:Oxford English Dictionary|Oxford English Dictionary]]'', the word may come from {{bor|es|nci|mallihuan||prisoner}}, however, linguist Jason D. Haugen finds no semantic basis for an etymological connection, suggesting that the phonetic similarity may be "a case of accidental [[homophony]]".<ref>{{cite-journal |last=Haugen |first=Jason D.|title=Borrowed Borrowings: Nahuatl Loan Words in English |journal=Lexis: e-Journal in English Lexicology |volume=3 |pages=63–106|issn=1951-6215|url=http://screcherche.univ-lyon3.fr/lexis/IMG/pdf/Lexis_3.pdf#page=63 |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20111001132105/http://screcherche.univ-lyon3.fr/lexis/IMG/pdf/Lexis_3.pdf#page=63|archivedate=October 1, 2011 |accessdate=3 April 2023}}</ref> Another theory derives it from {{bor|es|zh|[[麻]][[仁]][[花]]|tr=má rén huā||hemp seed flower}}, possibly itself originating as a loan from an earlier Semitic root ''[[*mrj]]'', meaning "hemp".<ref>Weston La Barre, (1980) "History and Ethnography of Cannabis", in Culture in Context, Selected writings. Durham NC: Duke University Press</ref>

